Study these 5 pairs of UML design solutions. For each task, the first solution demonstrates better design practices.

=== EXAMPLE 1 ===
Task: A smart city platform manages different types of users (Operators, Analysts, and Engineers) who monitor and control traffic signals across the city. Each user can hold multiple roles, such as monitoring data and managing maintenance tasks. The system maintains a single shared traffic control instance to coordinate all signals and prevent inconsistent updates.

GOOD:
@startuml
abstract class User {
    - id : int
    - name : string
    + {abstract} getInfo()
    + {abstract} addRole(spec)
    + {abstract} getRole(spec)
}

abstract class UserRole extends User {
    + getInfo()
    + addRole(spec)
    + getRole(spec)
}

class UserCore extends User {
    + getInfo()
    + addRole(spec)
    + getRole(spec)
}

class Operator extends UserRole {
    + monitorSignal()
}

class Engineer extends UserRole {
    + performMaintenance()
}

class Analyst extends UserRole {
    + analyzeTrafficData()
}

class TrafficController {
    - static instance : TrafficController
    - signals : List<string>
    + getInstance() : TrafficController
    + manageSignal(location : string, status : string)
}

UserRole --> UserCore : core
UserCore o-- UserRole : role
@enduml

BAD:
@startuml
class User {
    - id : int
    - name : string
    - role : string
    + getInfo()
}

class Operator {
    - name : string
    + monitorSignal()
}

class Engineer {
    - name : string
    + performMaintenance()
}

class Analyst {
    - name : string
    + analyzeTrafficData()
}

class TrafficController {
    - signals : List<string>
    + manageSignal(location : string, status : string)
}

User --> TrafficController
Operator --> TrafficController
Engineer --> TrafficController
Analyst --> TrafficController
@enduml

=== EXAMPLE 2 ===
Task: A company wants to build a music streaming platform that supports multiple platforms (Web, Mobile, Desktop). Each platform must have its own style of player, interface, and storage system. The app should be able to generate the correct set of related components depending on the platform being used.

GOOD:
@startuml
abstract class Player {
    + play(song : string)
}

abstract class Storage {
    + saveData(data : string)
}

abstract class UI {
    + render()
}

abstract class PlatformFactory {
    + {abstract} createPlayer() : Player
    + {abstract} createStorage() : Storage
    + {abstract} createUI() : UI
}

class WebFactory extends PlatformFactory {
    + createPlayer() : Player
    + createStorage() : Storage
    + createUI() : UI
}

class MobileFactory extends PlatformFactory {
    + createPlayer() : Player
    + createStorage() : Storage
    + createUI() : UI
}

class DesktopFactory extends PlatformFactory {
    + createPlayer() : Player
    + createStorage() : Storage
    + createUI() : UI
}

class WebPlayer extends Player {
    + play(song : string)
}

class MobilePlayer extends Player {
    + play(song : string)
}

class DesktopPlayer extends Player {
    + play(song : string)
}

class WebStorage extends Storage {
    + saveData(data : string)
}

class MobileStorage extends Storage {
    + saveData(data : string)
}

class DesktopStorage extends Storage {
    + saveData(data : string)
}

class WebUI extends UI {
    + render()
}

class MobileUI extends UI {
    + render()
}

class DesktopUI extends UI {
    + render()
}

Client --> Player
Client --> Storage
Client --> UI
Client --> PlatformFactory
@enduml

BAD:
@startuml
class WebPlayer {
    + play(song : string)
}

class MobilePlayer {
    + play(song : string)
}

class DesktopPlayer {
    + play(song : string)
}

class WebStorage {
    + saveData(data : string)
}

class MobileStorage {
    + saveData(data : string)
}

class DesktopStorage {
    + saveData(data : string)
}

class WebUI {
    + render()
}

class MobileUI {
    + render()
}

class DesktopUI {
    + render()
}

class Client {
    + platform : string
    + startApp()
}

Client --> WebPlayer
Client --> MobilePlayer
Client --> DesktopPlayer
Client --> WebStorage
Client --> MobileStorage
Client --> DesktopStorage
Client --> WebUI
Client --> MobileUI
Client --> DesktopUI
@enduml

=== EXAMPLE 3 ===
Task: A file management application needs to represent hierarchical data where both individual files and folders can be managed uniformly. Each folder can contain files or subfolders, and operations like calculating total size or displaying structure should be performed recursively across all elements.

GOOD:
@startuml
abstract class FileSystemComponent {
    + {abstract} getName() : string
    + {abstract} getSize() : int
    + {abstract} display(indent : int)
    + {abstract} add(FileSystemComponent)
    + {abstract} remove(FileSystemComponent)
}

class FileLeaf extends FileSystemComponent {
    - name : string
    - size : int
    + getName() : string
    + getSize() : int
    + display(indent : int)
}

class FolderComposite extends FileSystemComponent {
    - name : string
    - components : List<FileSystemComponent>
    + add(c : FileSystemComponent)
    + remove(c : FileSystemComponent)
    + getName() : string
    + getSize() : int
    + display(indent : int)
}

FolderComposite o-- FileSystemComponent
@enduml

BAD:
@startuml
class File {
    - name : string
    - size : int
    + getName() : string
    + getSize() : int
}

class Folder {
    - name : string
    - files : List<File>
    - subFolders : List<Folder>
    + addFile(f : File)
    + addFolder(f : Folder)
    + display()
}

Folder --> File
@enduml

=== EXAMPLE 4 ===
Task: A hospital system manages staff members who can have multiple responsibilities such as Doctor, Nurse, or Administrator. The system must support adding new staff roles dynamically while allowing the creation of different staff categories. The creation logic for staff members must be centralized, ensuring consistency in instantiation.

GOOD:
@startuml
abstract class Staff {
    - id : int
    - name : string
    + {abstract} getInfo()
    + {abstract} addRole(spec)
    + {abstract} getRole(spec)
}

abstract class StaffRole extends Staff {
    + getInfo()
    + addRole(spec)
    + getRole(spec)
}

class StaffCore extends Staff {
    + getInfo()
    + addRole(spec)
    + getRole(spec)
}

class Doctor extends StaffRole {
    + treatPatient()
}

class Nurse extends StaffRole {
    + updateVitals()
}

class Administrator extends StaffRole {
    + manageRecords()
}

abstract class StaffFactory {
    + {abstract} createStaff(type : string) : Staff
}

class HospitalStaffFactory extends StaffFactory {
    + createStaff(type : string) : Staff
}

StaffRole --> StaffCore : core
StaffCore o-- StaffRole : role
StaffFactory o-- Staff
@enduml

BAD:
@startuml
class Staff {
    - id : int
    - name : string
    - type : string
    + getInfo()
}

class Doctor {
    + treatPatient()
}

class Nurse {
    + updateVitals()
}

class Administrator {
    + manageRecords()
}

class Hospital {
    - doctors : List<Doctor>
    - nurses : List<Nurse>
    - admins : List<Administrator>
    + hireDoctor()
    + hireNurse()
    + hireAdmin()
}
@enduml

=== EXAMPLE 5 ===
Task: An online publishing platform manages articles that can be enhanced with additional features such as tagging, translation, and highlighting. The system should allow features to be added dynamically without modifying the core article class, supporting flexible combinations of features.

GOOD:
@startuml
abstract class Article {
    + {abstract} display() : string
}

class BasicArticle extends Article {
    - content : string
    + display() : string
}

abstract class ArticleDecorator extends Article {
    # article : Article
    + display() : string
}

class TaggedArticle extends ArticleDecorator {
    + display() : string
}

class TranslatedArticle extends ArticleDecorator {
    + display() : string
}

class HighlightedArticle extends ArticleDecorator {
    + display() : string
}

ArticleDecorator o-- Article
@enduml

BAD:
@startuml
class Article {
    - content : string
    + display() : string
}

class TaggedArticle {
    - content : string
    - tags : List<string>
    + display() : string
}

class TranslatedArticle {
    - content : string
    - language : string
    + display() : string
}

class HighlightedArticle {
    - content : string
    + display() : string
}
@enduml

